;;; 80-objects.slat — Motoi Scheme Reference · Appendix / Chapter 80 ·
;;; Objects from primitives: decompose a real thing into shapes, build it up
;;; rung by rung, combine the results, and run the map backward. A LEVEL-3
;;; (apply) chapter.
;;;
;;; Written 2026-07-29. This is the payoff appendix for the drawing libraries in
;;; chapters 73–78 and the stack map in 79-shape-stack.slat. Where 79 asks WHICH
;;; layer, this asks HOW TO BUILD A THING: a building is squares inside squares,
;;; a tree is a crown above a trunk, a snowman is three discs stacked. The skill
;;; is compositional — decompose the object into primitives you have, then
;;; combine them until the picture is the thing.
;;;
;;; Two directions, both taught here. FORWARD: words -> primitives -> picture
;;; ("draw me a tree"). REVERSE: picture -> named parts ("what is this made of?").
;;; A model that can do both owns the map, not one side of it.
;;;
;;; Every code block RUNS through ./bin/motoi (verified 2026-07-29); painting
;;; blocks are prefixed with (set-mode 'sd) and print nothing.
;;;
;;; Made by Alfred Borden.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "Objects from primitives — a level-3 apply chapter: decompose, build up, combine, reverse.")

(chapter :number "80" :library "objects" :kind "meta-chapter"
  :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :author "Alfred Borden"
  :axis "sensation"
  :bloom "apply"
  :read-order-hint "read after 73-draw through 78-svg-path and 79-shape-stack"
  :prose
"# Objects from primitives

Made by Alfred Borden.

## The whole idea in one line

A picture of a thing is that thing DECOMPOSED into primitives you already have,
then COMBINED until it reads. You do not need a 'building' verb to draw a
building — you need to see that a building is squares inside squares, and you
already have squares. That seeing is the skill this chapter drills: take a real
object, name the shapes it is made of, and stack them with the combinators from
chapter 75. Nothing here is a new primitive. It is a new way of LOOKING at the
primitives you were given.

This is the same move a person makes to sketch in ASCII or SVG: no 'cat' glyph
exists, so you reason a cat down into a circle head, two triangle ears, a body.
Motoi draws the same way. The reference has taught the verbs; this appendix
teaches the composition — how to get from a WORD to a PICTURE, and back.

## Decompose before you draw

Before a single call, answer three questions about the object:

1. **What primitives is it made of?** A tree: a circle (crown) and a rectangle
   (trunk). A snowman: three circles. A house: a square and a triangle. Name the
   shapes; do not yet worry about placing them.
2. **How are they arranged?** Stacked (`above`), side by side (`beside`), or one
   on top of another (`overlay`)? A tree is a crown ABOVE a trunk. A building's
   floors are squares OVERLAID, largest behind. Windows are a grid — `above` of
   `beside`.
3. **Which layer?** By chapter 79's diagnosis: reusable parts you will repeat →
   image algebra; a hole or a carve → SDF; an organic bloom → superformula; one
   mark now → raster. Most everyday objects live in the image-algebra layer,
   because objects are made of REPEATED, MOVABLE parts.

Answer those three and the code almost writes itself. The hard part of drawing
is never the syntax; it is the decomposition.

## Build up, rung by rung

Never write the finished object in one breath. Climb to it, and make every rung
run — the build-up shape from chapter 00g, applied to a picture. Here is a
building, one rung at a time:

  rung 1:  (img/square 8 'solid 7)                    ; the innermost room, a value
  rung 2:  (overlay (img/square 8 'solid 7)
                    (img/square 16 'outline 6))       ; a room inside a wall
  rung 3:  add a third square                          ; wall inside a wall
  rung 4:  wrap the whole ladder in a function of n     ; a building of any depth

Each rung is a whole program that runs on its own, and each rung is the last one
wrapped in one more square. By rung 4 you have not just A building — you have a
`building` function that takes a floor count and returns the picture. That jump,
from a fixed drawing to a PARAMETERIZED one, is the point of building up: the
object becomes a recipe, and a recipe can be reused, resized, and combined.

## Combine — a forest from a tree

Once a thing is a recipe, more of it is free. A tree is a crown above a trunk; a
forest is that tree, made a few sizes, set `beside` itself. The combinators are
the same ones that built the tree — composition does not stop at the object
boundary. This is what 'draw me some trees, now put them in the scene' means in
practice: build one asset well, then let `map` and `apply beside` (or `above`,
or `overlay`) turn one asset into a population.

  (define (tree crown) (above (img/circle crown 'solid 11)
                              (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5)))
  (apply beside (map tree (list 8 12 10 14)))          ; four trees, one row

The forest is not a new idea; it is the tree idea applied four times and set in a
row. Assets compose into scenes the same way shapes compose into assets. It is
composition all the way up.

## Run it backward — the reverse skill

Drawing is only half the map. The other half is looking at a picture and NAMING
what it is made of — the reverse direction from chapter 00g's reversals, now
about objects instead of folds. Two ways to practice it:

**Measure the parts.** An image is a value you can interrogate. Given a tree
built as `(above crown trunk)`, `image-width` and `image-height` report its
bounding box, and the width equals the WIDER of crown and trunk — which tells you,
without seeing pixels, that the crown (a disc) is the wide part and the trunk (a
thin rectangle) is the tall part. Measurement recovers structure.

**Name the decomposition.** Given the expression
`(above (img/circle 12 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5))`, say in words:
'a green disc stacked above a brown rectangle — a tree, crown over trunk.' Given
the words, write the expression. Running both directions on the same object is
what proves the model understands the object, not just one phrasing of it.

The forward skill draws the thing. The reverse skill reads the thing. A drawer
that cannot read cannot correct itself; a reader that cannot draw cannot help.
The appendix insists on both because the object is the same object from either
end, and knowing it means being able to turn the crank in either direction.

## A small catalogue of decompositions

These are not the only right answers — they are worked examples of the move. Each
is a real object seen as primitives and an arrangement.

- **Building** — squares OVERLAID, largest behind, smallest (a lit room) in front.
- **Tree** — a circle crown ABOVE a rectangle trunk.
- **Snowman** — three circles ABOVE each other, growing downward.
- **House** — a triangle roof ABOVE a square body (painted directly, or as images).
- **Window wall** — a grid: `above` of two `beside` pairs of outline squares.
- **Car** — a rectangle body with two circle wheels overlaid below.
- **Flower** — a superformula bloom with a small disc overlaid at its centre.
- **Sun** — a many-lobed superformula stroke, or a disc with radiating spokes.

Learn the move on these eight and the ninth object is a decomposition you have
never seen but can do: name its shapes, choose an arrangement, build up, combine.
That is the whole of drawing-from-thought — near-infinite pictures from a small
fixed set of primitives, because the primitives were never the limit. The
composition was the language, and the language has no last sentence."
)

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; Machine-readable object records. Each carries a decomposition, a build-up
;; ladder (every rung runnable), a combination, and BOTH directions (forward
;; words->code and reverse code->words). Painting programs are prefixed with
;; (set-mode 'sd) and print nothing. Axis: sensation. Bloom: apply.
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(object
  :name "building" :reads-as "squares encased in each other"
  :primitives ("img/square") :arrangement "overlay, largest behind"
  :layer "image-algebra"
  :ladder (
    (:rung 1 :label "one room" :code "(image-width (img/square 8 'solid 7))" :expect "8")
    (:rung 2 :label "room in a wall" :code "(image? (overlay (img/square 8 'solid 7) (img/square 16 'outline 6)))" :expect "#t")
    (:rung 3 :label "walls within walls" :code "(image? (overlay (img/square 8 'solid 7) (img/square 16 'outline 6) (img/square 24 'outline 6) (img/square 32 'outline 5)))" :expect "#t")
    (:rung 4 :label "a building of any depth" :code "(define (building n)\n  (apply overlay (map (lambda (i)\n                       (img/square (* 8 (+ i 1)) (if (= i 0) 'solid 'outline) (- 8 i)))\n                     (iota n))))\n(image-width (building 4))" :expect "32"))
  :forward (:prompt "draw a building — squares inside squares"
    :code "(set-mode 'sd) (draw-image (overlay (img/square 10 'solid 7) (img/square 20 'outline 6) (img/square 30 'outline 5)) 100 100)" :expect "")
  :reverse (:code "(overlay (img/square 8 'solid 7) (img/square 16 'outline 6) (img/square 24 'outline 5))"
    :read-as "three concentric squares, solid room innermost — a building seen face-on"
    :measure "(image-width (overlay (img/square 8 'solid 7) (img/square 24 'outline 5)))" :measure-expect "24")
  :note "The whole object is ONE overlay expression. That is the win over four scattered raster calls: the building is a value."
)

(object
  :name "tree" :reads-as "a crown above a trunk"
  :primitives ("img/circle" "img/rectangle") :arrangement "above"
  :layer "image-algebra"
  :ladder (
    (:rung 1 :label "the crown" :code "(image-height (img/circle 12 'solid 11))" :expect "24")
    (:rung 2 :label "crown over trunk" :code "(image-height (above (img/circle 12 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5)))" :expect "41"))
  :forward (:prompt "draw a tree"
    :code "(set-mode 'sd) (draw-image (above (img/circle 12 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5)) 100 100)" :expect "")
  :reverse (:code "(above (img/circle 12 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5))"
    :read-as "a green disc stacked over a brown rectangle — a tree, crown over trunk"
    :measure "(list (image-width (above (img/circle 12 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5))) (image-height (above (img/circle 12 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5))))" :measure-expect "(25 41)")
  :note "Width comes from the wider part (the crown disc, 25); height sums the stack. Measurement recovers which part is which."
)

(object
  :name "forest" :reads-as "one tree, made a few sizes, set in a row"
  :primitives ("img/circle" "img/rectangle") :arrangement "beside (of trees)"
  :layer "image-algebra"
  :note "The combination move: an asset applied N times becomes a scene."
  :ladder (
    (:rung 1 :label "the tree recipe" :code "(define (tree crown) (above (img/circle crown 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5)))\n(image? (tree 10))" :expect "#t")
    (:rung 2 :label "a row of four" :code "(define (tree crown) (above (img/circle crown 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5)))\n(image-width (apply beside (map tree (list 8 12 10 14))))" :expect "62"))
  :forward (:prompt "draw me some trees and put them in a row"
    :code "(set-mode 'sd) (define (tree crown) (above (img/circle crown 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5)))\n(draw-image (apply beside (map tree (list 8 12 10 14))) 120 100)" :expect "")
  :reverse (:code "(apply beside (map (lambda (c) (above (img/circle c 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5))) (list 8 12 10)))"
    :read-as "three trees of different crown sizes set side by side — a little forest")
)

(object
  :name "snowman" :reads-as "three discs stacked, growing downward"
  :primitives ("img/circle") :arrangement "above"
  :layer "image-algebra"
  :ladder (
    (:rung 1 :label "the head" :code "(image-height (img/circle 6 'solid 12))" :expect "12")
    (:rung 2 :label "head and body" :code "(image-height (above (img/circle 6 'solid 12) (img/circle 9 'solid 12)))" :expect "30")
    (:rung 3 :label "three stacked" :code "(image-height (above (img/circle 6 'solid 12) (img/circle 9 'solid 12) (img/circle 12 'solid 12)))" :expect "54"))
  :forward (:prompt "draw a snowman"
    :code "(set-mode 'sd) (draw-image/center (above (img/circle 6 'solid 12) (img/circle 9 'solid 12) (img/circle 12 'solid 12)) 160 120)" :expect "")
  :reverse (:code "(above (img/circle 6 'solid 12) (img/circle 9 'solid 12) (img/circle 12 'solid 12))"
    :read-as "three white discs, smallest on top, largest at the bottom — a snowman")
  :note "Radii 6/9/12 grow downward, so the stack widens toward the base the way a snowman sits."
)

(object
  :name "house" :reads-as "a triangle roof above a square body"
  :primitives ("triangle-fill" "polygon-fill") :arrangement "roof above body"
  :layer "raster"
  :note "Drawn straight to the framebuffer at chosen pixels — layer 1, because it is one placed mark, not a reusable asset."
  :ladder (
    (:rung 1 :label "the body" :code "(set-mode 'sd) (polygon-fill (list (list 15 45) (list 65 45) (list 65 90) (list 15 90)) 8)" :expect "")
    (:rung 2 :label "roof on body" :code "(set-mode 'sd) (polygon-fill (list (list 15 45) (list 65 45) (list 65 90) (list 15 90)) 8) (triangle-fill 40 15 15 45 65 45 3)" :expect ""))
  :forward (:prompt "draw a house"
    :code "(set-mode 'sd) (triangle-fill 40 15 15 45 65 45 3) (polygon-fill (list (list 15 45) (list 65 45) (list 65 90) (list 15 90)) 8)" :expect "")
  :reverse (:prompt "a filled triangle sitting on top of a filled square, sharing an edge"
    :read-as "a house — pitched roof over a square body")
)

(object
  :name "car" :reads-as "a rectangle body with two circle wheels"
  :primitives ("polygon-fill" "ellipse-fill") :arrangement "wheels overlaid below the body"
  :layer "raster"
  :ladder (
    (:rung 1 :label "the body" :code "(set-mode 'sd) (polygon-fill (list (list 20 60) (list 100 60) (list 100 80) (list 20 80)) 9)" :expect "")
    (:rung 2 :label "add two wheels" :code "(set-mode 'sd) (polygon-fill (list (list 20 60) (list 100 60) (list 100 80) (list 20 80)) 9) (ellipse-fill 40 80 8 8 0) (ellipse-fill 80 80 8 8 0)" :expect ""))
  :forward (:prompt "draw a car"
    :code "(set-mode 'sd) (polygon-fill (list (list 20 60) (list 100 60) (list 100 80) (list 20 80)) 9) (ellipse-fill 40 80 8 8 0) (ellipse-fill 80 80 8 8 0)" :expect "")
  :reverse (:prompt "a wide rectangle with two dark discs under its two ends"
    :read-as "a car — a body on two wheels")
)

(object
  :name "flower" :reads-as "a superformula bloom with a disc centre"
  :primitives ("superformula-fill" "ellipse-fill") :arrangement "centre overlaid on petals"
  :layer "superformula + raster"
  :note "The organic layer draws petals from one equation; a raster disc caps the centre. Two layers, one object."
  :forward (:prompt "draw a flower with five petals"
    :code "(set-mode 'sd) (superformula-fill 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 90 12) (ellipse-fill 160 120 8 8 10)" :expect "")
  :reverse (:prompt "a five-lobed filled bloom with a small solid disc at its middle"
    :read-as "a flower — petals from a superformula, a raster disc for the centre")
  :ladder (
    (:rung 1 :label "the petals alone" :code "(set-mode 'sd) (superformula-fill 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 90 12)" :expect "")
    (:rung 2 :label "cap the centre" :code "(set-mode 'sd) (superformula-fill 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 90 12) (ellipse-fill 160 120 8 8 10)" :expect ""))
)

(object
  :name "gear" :reads-as "a ring with a bite carved out"
  :primitives ("sdf/circle" "sdf/box" "sdf-subtract") :arrangement "carve a hub and a slot from a disc"
  :layer "sdf"
  :note "When the object has a HOLE, drop to the SDF layer — carving is subtraction, one call per bite (chapter 79, layer 4)."
  :ladder (
    (:rung 1 :label "a disc" :code "(sdf? (sdf/circle 100 100 34))" :expect "#t")
    (:rung 2 :label "hollow the hub" :code "(sdf? (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 100 100 34) (sdf/circle 100 100 14)))" :expect "#t")
    (:rung 3 :label "cut a slot" :code "(sdf? (sdf-subtract (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 100 100 34) (sdf/circle 100 100 14)) (sdf/box 100 60 6 40)))" :expect "#t"))
  :forward (:prompt "draw a gear-like ring with a slot"
    :code "(set-mode 'sd) (sdf-fill (sdf-subtract (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 100 100 34) (sdf/circle 100 100 14)) (sdf/box 100 60 6 40)) 3)" :expect "")
  :reverse (:code "(sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 100 100 34) (sdf/circle 100 100 14))"
    :read-as "a large disc minus a smaller concentric disc — a ring / washer")
)

(reversal-drill
  :name "words-and-code-both-ways"
  :note "The map from object to primitives runs in both directions; running both proves understanding."
  :programs (
    (:direction "words->code" :prompt "a crown above a trunk"
     :code "(image? (above (img/circle 12 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5)))" :expect "#t"
     :read-as "a tree")
    (:direction "code->words" :code "(image-height (above (img/circle 6 'solid 12) (img/circle 9 'solid 12) (img/circle 12 'solid 12)))" :expect "54"
     :read-as "three discs stacked and growing downward — a snowman; its height is the three diameters summed")
    (:direction "measure->structure" :code "(let ((tree (above (img/circle 12 'solid 11) (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5))))\n  (> (image-width tree) (image-width (img/rectangle 6 16 'solid 5))))" :expect "#t"
     :read-as "the picture is wider than its trunk alone — so the crown is the wide part, recovered by measurement"))
)
